1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,080
Listen to a talk in a history class.

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:11,210
Today we'll examine the Silk Road, an ancient network of trade routes that connected the east and west.

3
00:00:11,210 --> 00:00:13,598
The Silk Road was not a single road,

4
00:00:13,598 --> 00:00:19,237
but a series of interconnected paths that facilitated the exchange of goods, ideas

5
00:00:19,237 --> 00:00:23,050
and cultures from China to the Mediterranean Sea.

6
00:00:23,050 --> 00:00:28,791
Originating in the Han Dynasty around the 2nd century BCE, the Silk Road was

7
00:00:28,791 --> 00:00:31,200
named after the lucrative trade in silk

8
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:35,210
which was one of the most sought-after commodities in the western world.

9
00:00:35,210 --> 00:00:38,290
However, it wasn't just silk that was traded.

10
00:00:38,290 --> 00:00:46,170
Merchants carried spices, precious metals, textiles and other valuable items across vast distances.

11
00:00:46,170 --> 00:00:52,190
The Silk Road also played a critical role in the transmission of knowledge and culture.

12
00:00:52,190 --> 00:01:00,230
It enabled the spread of technologies such as paper making and gunpowder, as well as artistic and religious influences.

13
00:01:00,230 --> 00:01:07,050
For instance, Buddhism spread from India to China and other parts of Asia via the Silk Road.

14
00:01:07,050 --> 00:01:12,705
The decline of the Silk Road began in the 15th century with the rise of maritime trade routes

15
00:01:12,705 --> 00:01:14,751
and the fall of the Mongol Empire,

16
00:01:14,751 --> 00:01:18,200
which had provided stability and security for the merchants.

17
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:25,826
Although it no longer exists, the legacy of the Silk Road endures, highlighting the importance of cultural

18
00:01:25,826 --> 00:01:30,060
and economic exchanges in shaping human history.
